STATUS: Generate one standalone decorative frame overlay for the Urkaaaz
campaign map screen, matching the newly attached campaign-screen reference.
This image replaces all earlier campaign-screen references; ignore the
previous red-banner concept. This is not a complete screenshot, map
background, or flattened UI. Android renders text, chapter cards, level
markers, and progress separately.

## Reference input

- Use only the newest attached campaign-screen image as the visual reference.
  Match its outer parchment-and-wood frame, deep-blue Orc pennants, gold-edged
  rectangular Back-button mount, corner ornaments, and upper-left title
  plaque. Ignore all earlier campaign-screen images and designs.
  The reference also shows a bottom-center Start Level button; do not include
  that button in this frame asset.
- Attach
  `assets_src/campaign/chapter-maps/campaign_chapter_01_green_frontier_map.png`
  to check the frame's colors and transparent opening against the real map.
  Do not include or repaint any of the map in the output.
## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG:
  `campaign_map_frame_overlay.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/campaign/ui/`.
- Canvas: exactly 1920 × 1080 pixels, landscape 16:9, RGBA.
- The canvas must be transparent everywhere except for the decorative frame
  and its attached ornaments. Use genuine alpha, not a checkerboard or a
  painted transparent-looking center.
- Extend the frame to the canvas edges. Do not leave an outer transparent
  gutter that could reveal another screen behind the campaign screen.
- Return the frame overlay only. Do not generate a screenshot, mockup, map,
  or screen background.
- Do not add nine-patch guide marks. Keep long edge sections visually simple
  enough to be sliced or nine-patched later without stretching the detailed
  corner ornaments.

## Frame design

- Create a rugged, hand-crafted Orc campaign-map frame: dark timber, worn
  parchment, hammered iron, restrained brass details, and warm torchlit
  highlights. Match the attached concept's rich painterly fantasy finish.
- Make the border irregular and tactile, with carved or riveted corner
  details. Keep the perimeter coherent as one frame and avoid delicate
  filigree that will disappear at phone size.
- Include a fixed, empty parchment title plaque integrated into the upper-left
  frame area. Add a blank deep-blue ribbon above it for the live “CAMPAIGN”
  label. Keep the plaque and ribbon free of text; Android will place the
  campaign title, chapter title, and progress as live UI.
- Include a blank, gold-edged rectangular mount for the Back button at the
  upper-left corner. Android will draw the arrow; do not paint the arrow into
  this asset.
- Add a deep-blue Orc pennant with a simple ivory skull emblem along the left
  frame, and a smaller matching blue pennant near the lower-right corner, as
  shown in the new reference. These are frame decorations, not level markers
  or status cues.
- Add modest rolled-map, compass, or leather details in the lower corners,
  but keep them attached to the frame and away from the playable map area.
- Leave the upper-right area free of fixed panels so Android can place a
  variable number of chapter cards there.

## Transparent opening and safe areas

- Keep the large central opening fully transparent so the campaign map can be
  drawn behind it without tint, haze, vignette, texture, or color cast.
- Confine most frame artwork to the outer 5–7% of the canvas.
- The upper-left Back-button mount and title plaque may extend inward to about
  34% of the canvas width and 21% of its height. Keep them in the map's empty
  upper-left area.
- Keep corner ornaments compact and clear of all map landmarks. In
  particular, do not intrude toward the southwest level 1 anchor, which is
  far below the title plaque.
- Do not place decoration across the map route or the ten level-marker
  locations. The frame must not force any anchor to move.
- Make edges cleanly antialiased with no colored fringe, detached pixels, or
  shadow extending into the transparent map opening.

## Separation from screen UI and map art

- This is a decorative overlay, not a full campaign-screen composition.
- Do not draw the map, paths, terrain, water, sky, forts, bosses, characters,
  or level markers.
- Do not draw chapter cards or thumbnails; these will be separate reusable UI
  components.
- Do not draw numbers, words, letters, labels, progress bars, locks,
  checkmarks, buttons, selectable states, status colors, or a Back arrow.
- Do not bake the screenshot's chapter text, 1 / 10 progress, current-level
  glow, level states, or any other text into the plaque or ribbon. Keep all
  referenced screen content out of the frame artwork.
- The blank Back-button mount is allowed, but do not paint a Back arrow or
  other icon into it.
- Do not add circular frames, marker rings, or opaque panels over the central
  map area.

## Negative prompt

opaque background, map, landscape, terrain, path, river, fortress, castle,
boss, character, marker, icon, numbered pin, circle, status ring, lock,
checkmark, text, letters, numbers, labels, title, logo, UI, button, Back
arrow, chapter card, thumbnail, progress bar, watermark, signature,
checkerboard, haze, vignette, opaque center, colored center, jagged alpha,
colored fringe, detached shadow, stretched ornaments, human medieval castle,
clean European stonework, red Orc banner, red faction pennant

## Review checklist

- Confirm the image is exactly 1920 × 1080 RGBA and is transparent outside the
  frame and attached ornaments.
- Composite it over the actual Green Frontier map and confirm the map remains
  vivid and unobscured through the large central opening.
- Confirm the upper-left plaque, ribbon, and Back-button mount are blank and
  the upper-right area remains free for dynamic chapter cards.
- Confirm the frame does not cover level anchors or require moving landmarks.
- Preview at phone display size; verify the wood, parchment, ironwork, and
  deep-blue Orc pennants remain readable without dominating the map.
- Confirm the result is only the overlay asset, not a flattened screenshot.
- Do not import it into Android until the image is reviewed and approved.
